  "attributionNote": "Mandukya Karika, as Heritage IITKanpur's Scripture Server catalogues it (GKA), on its own shelf.",
  "description": "The Māṇḍūkya Kārikā is Gauḍapāda's Sanskrit verse exposition of the brief Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad: 215 kārikās in four chapters (prakaraṇas). Tradition names Gauḍapāda the teacher of Śaṅkara's teacher. Its subject is the Self, examined through the syllable Om and the three states of waking, dream and deep sleep, with the fourth, turīya, that underlies them. Its purpose is to show that nothing has truly come into being (ajāti), so that the seeker rests in the non-dual. Regarded as the earliest surviving systematic work of Advaita Vedānta and read with Śaṅkara's commentary, it is for one who has studied the Upaniṣads and wants their teaching argued step by step; other schools read the Upaniṣad differently. The summary above is the one Heritage IITKanpur's Scripture Server publishes for the text, and the server's own metadata records it as AI-assisted (claude-opus-5-5); an editor's note for the work itself is still to be written.",
